statement.p:
 Consider the matrix
	 A = \left[\begin{array}{rr}
	7 \amp 6 \\
	6 \amp -2 \\
	\end{array}\right]

	and the vector \vec{v}=\twovec{2}{1}.  We find that

	  A\vec{v} = \left[\begin{array}{rr}
	  7 \amp 6 \\
	  6 \amp -2 \\
	  \end{array}\right]
	  \twovec{2}{1}
	  =
	  \twovec{20}{10}
	  =10\twovec{2}{1}
	  =10\vec{v}
	.
	In other words, A\vec{v} = 10\vec{v}, which says that
	\vec{v} is an eigenvector of the matrix A with
	associated eigenvalue \lambda = 10.

 Similarly, if \vec{w} = \twovec{-1}{2}, we find that

	  A\vec{w} = \left[\begin{array}{rr}
	  7 \amp 6 \\
	  6 \amp -2 \\
	  \end{array}\right]
	  \twovec{-1}{2}
	  =
	  \twovec{5}{-10}
	  =-5\twovec{-1}{2}
	  =-5\vec{w}
	.
	Here again, we have A\vec{w} = -5\vec{w} showing that
	\vec{w} is an eigenvector of A with associated
	eigenvalue \lambda=-5.

subsection.p:
Let's consider the ideas we saw in the activity in some more
   depth.  To be an eigenvector of A, the vector \vec{v}
   must satisfy A\vec{v} = \lambda\vec{v} for some scalar
   \lambda.  This means that \vec{v} and A\vec{v}
   are scalar multiples of each other so they must lie on
   the same line.

sidebyside.p:
For instance, if we consider the vector
\vec{x}=\vec{v_1}-2\vec{v_2}, we find that

  \begin{aligned}
  A\vec{x}\amp{}={}A(\vec{v_1} - 2\vec{v_2}) \\
  A\vec{x}\amp{}={} 3\vec{v_1} + 2\vec{v_2}
  \end{aligned}

as seen in the figure.

subsection.p:
First, we compute

     \begin{aligned}
     A\vec{v_1} =
     \left[\begin{array}{rr}
     0.8 \amp 0.6 \\
     0.2 \amp 0.4 \\
     \end{array}\right]
     \twovec{3}{1}
     \amp {}={}
     \twovec{3}{1}
     = 1\vec{v_1}
     \\
     \\
     A\vec{v_2} =
     \left[\begin{array}{rr}
     0.8 \amp 0.6 \\
     0.2 \amp 0.4 \\
     \end{array}\right]
     \twovec{-1}{1}
     \amp {}={}
     \twovec{-0.2}{0.2}
     = 0.2\vec{v_2}\text{.} \\
     \end{aligned}

   This shows that \vec{v_1} is an eigenvector of A with
   eigenvalue \lambda_1 = 1 and \vec{v_2} is an
   eigenvector of A with eigenvalue \lambda_2=0.2.

To determine the distribution of cars on subsequent days, we
   will repeatedly multiply by A.  We find that

     \begin{aligned}
     \vec{x_1} = A\vec{x_0} \amp {}={}
     A(250\vec{v_1} - 250\vec{v_2}) = 250\vec{v_1} - (0.2)250\vec{v_2} \\
     \vec{x_2} = A\vec{x_1} \amp {}={}
     A(250\vec{v_1} - (0.2)250\vec{v_2}) = 250\vec{v_1} - (0.2)^2250\vec{v_2} \\
     \vec{x_3} = A\vec{x_2} \amp {}={}
     A(250\vec{v_1} - (0.2)^2250\vec{v_2}) = 250\vec{v_1} - (0.2)^3250\vec{v_2} \\
     \vec{x_4} = A\vec{x_3} \amp {}={}
     A(250\vec{v_1} - (0.2)^3250\vec{v_2}) = 250\vec{v_1} - (0.2)^4250\vec{v_2} \\
     \vec{x_5} = A\vec{x_4} \amp {}={}
     A(250\vec{v_1} - (0.2)^4250\vec{v_2}) = 250\vec{v_1} - (0.2)^5250\vec{v_2} \\
     \end{aligned}
   .
